        <div id="mah-vs-wh" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. mAh vs Wh: How Much Power Bank Do You Need for Camping?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power banks are sold by milliamp-hours, but milliamp-hours alone do not tell you how much energy a bank holds. Watt-hours do. The two numbers are linked by the battery's internal voltage, so a camper who understands both can compare banks honestly and size one for a trip instead of guessing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">mAh Is a Charge Figure, Wh Is an Energy Figure</h3>

          <p>
            A milliamp-hour rating describes charge at the cell's own voltage, which for lithium-ion cells is usually around 3.6 to 3.85 volts. To turn it into watt-hours, multiply mAh by that voltage and divide by 1,000. A hypothetical 10,000mAh bank at 3.7 volts holds about 37Wh. Because the cell voltage varies between makers, two banks with the same mAh can differ slightly in energy. When a maker prints Wh on the case or listing, trust that number over a conversion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Not All of It Reaches Your Phone</h3>

          <p>
            Every bank loses energy as it converts its cell voltage up to USB output, and your phone loses more as it charges its own battery. A common rule of thumb is that roughly two-thirds of the rated capacity ends up in your devices, though it varies with the bank, the cable and the temperature. That is why a 10,000mAh bank does not deliver three full charges to a phone with a 3,000mAh battery. If you need several full phone charges or want to top up a laptop, step up to a <a href="/best-high-capacity-power-bank/">high capacity power bank</a> and accept the extra weight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizing by Trip Length</h3>

          <p>
            For a single night or a day hike, 10,000mAh is plenty for a phone and a rechargeable headlamp. For a long weekend of three or four nights, 15,000 to 20,000mAh covers a phone used for photos and maps, plus a headlamp and earbuds. For a week or more without an outlet, or for a group, look at 20,000mAh and above, carry two banks, or plan a way to recharge. If you need to run anything with a plug, you have outgrown power banks. Plan that recharge around a folding <a href="/best-solar-charger/">solar charger</a> and a bank with USB-C input, and a week off-grid stops being a power problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Headlamps, Lights and GPS Units Use Less Than You Think</h3>

          <p>
            A rechargeable headlamp or small lantern typically has a battery far smaller than a phone's, so a bank refills it several times over. Satellite messengers and GPS units are similar. The phone, especially one used for navigation, photos and video with a weak signal, is almost always the device that drains your bank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ultralight, Rugged or High-Capacity: Which Camping Power Bank Type?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Camping power banks fall into three rough groups. Picking the group first narrows the choice faster than comparing every spec.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ultralight Backpacking Banks</h3>

          <p>
            These carry 10,000 to 20,000mAh in a light shell, often carbon fiber or a slim plastic body, with a basic water-resistance rating. The Nitecore NB10000 and NB20000 are the classic examples. They suit backpackers and anyone who carries power on their back all day. Day hikers who carry only an iPhone may prefer something even slimmer that snaps onto the back of the phone; our <a href="/best-power-bank-for-iphone/">power bank for iPhone</a> picks cover MagSafe and Qi2 models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rugged, Weatherproof Banks</h3>

          <p>
            Rugged banks add an IP67 rating, cushioned shells, port plugs, flashlights, clips and tripod threads. They weigh more per mAh, but they survive rain, river crossings, dust and drops. Choose one for paddling trips, winter camping or gear that lives in a truck.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">High-Capacity USB-C PD Banks</h3>

          <p>
            Large banks near the 100Wh airline limit, with 60 to 100W USB-C Power Delivery, charge laptops, tablets and cameras as well as phones. They are heavy and rarely weatherproof, so they suit car camping, overlanding and anyone working from camp.
          </p>

