        <div id="waterproof" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is a Power Bank Waterproof? IP Ratings Explained for Campers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most power banks have no water rating at all. A few outdoor models do, and the two-character IP code on the listing tells you exactly how much protection to expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Read an IP Code</h3>

          <p>
            The first digit rates protection from dust and solids, up to 6 for dust-tight. The second rates water, from 0 for none up to 7 for temporary immersion and 8 for longer immersion under conditions the maker sets. An X in place of the first digit means it was not tested for dust, not that it fails.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">IPX5: Rain and Splashes</h3>

          <p>
            IPX5 means protection against water jets from any direction. In camping terms, that covers rain, a wet tent floor and splashes, but not dropping the bank in a lake. The Nitecore NB20000 Gen 3 is rated IPX5.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">IPX7 and IP67: Brief Submersion</h3>

          <p>
            A 7 in the water position means the device survived immersion, typically to one meter for 30 minutes in the standard test. IP67 adds full dust protection. The Nitecore NB10000 Gen 4 is IPX7; the Goal Zero Venture 75, NESTOUT and INIU rugged banks are IP67.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Rating Does Not Promise</h3>

          <p>
            Ratings are tested on new units with plugs closed and in fresh water. Some banks only reach their rating with a port cover sealed, so close it. Never charge or plug in a wet bank: dry the ports first, and follow the maker's instructions on drying time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cold-Weather Camping: Why Power Banks Die Faster and What to Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion batteries hate the cold. Below freezing, the chemistry slows down, the usable capacity drops and the bank may shut off early, even though the energy is still there. Warm it up and much of that capacity comes back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep It Warm, Keep It Close</h3>

          <p>
            In cold weather, carry the bank in an inside jacket pocket during the day and keep it in your sleeping bag or a stuff sack near your body at night. Do the same with your phone. A bank that spends the night in a frozen tent vestibule will disappoint you in the morning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Charge a Frozen Battery</h3>

          <p>
            Charging lithium-ion cells below freezing can damage them. Most makers specify a charging temperature range, often starting around freezing. Let a cold bank warm to room temperature before you recharge it, and check your model's manual for its exact limits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan for Less Capacity in Winter</h3>

          <p>
            On winter trips, size up. A bank that comfortably covers a summer weekend may fall short in sub-freezing conditions. Carrying a little extra capacity, and keeping it warm, is the simplest fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backpacking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Best Power Bank for Backpacking: Weight vs Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            For backpackers, the right power bank is the lightest one that covers the trip, and a little more. Every extra ounce of capacity you do not use is weight carried for nothing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compare Ounces per 10,000mAh</h3>

          <p>
            Divide the weight by the capacity to compare banks fairly. By the listings, the Nitecore NB10000 Gen 4 is 4.94 ounces for 10,000mAh and the NB20000 Gen 3 is 10.26 ounces for 20,000mAh, while the rugged INIU is 6.9 ounces for 10,000mAh. Toughness and features cost weight, and that is a fair trade only if you need them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weekend Trips</h3>

          <p>
            On a two- or three-night trip, a 10,000mAh bank under six ounces is usually enough for a phone in airplane mode, a headlamp and a satellite messenger. Leave the cable spares and wall charger in the car.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thru-Hikes and Resupply</h3>

          <p>
            On long trails, you recharge in town every few days, so recharge speed matters as much as capacity. A bank with fast USB-C input and pass-through charging fills up during a short stop and lets you charge your phone from the same outlet. Many long-distance hikers carry a 20,000mAh bank for the longest stretches between towns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Do You Need a Solar Power Bank for Camping?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Solar sounds like the obvious answer for camping, but the small panel on a solar power bank collects very little energy. For most trips, a well-sized regular bank does more. Our guide to the <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">best solar power banks</a> covers the details; here is the short version.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In Panels Are a Trickle</h3>

          <p>
            The panel on a pocket-sized bank is tiny compared with the battery behind it, so it can take days of direct sun to refill. Treat a built-in panel as an emergency top-up, not a charging plan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Folding Panel Plus a Bank Works</h3>

          <p>
            If you are out for a week or more, a folding USB solar panel paired with a regular power bank recharges far faster. Hang the panel on your pack or set it in the sun at camp, charge the bank during the day and charge your devices from the bank at night. Banks like the Goal Zero Venture 75 list solar-panel charging through USB-C. The <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank guide</a> ranks panels and pairings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Solar Is Worth It</h3>

          <p>
            Solar pays off on long trips without outlets, base camps in sunny climates and emergency kits. For a weekend, the extra weight usually buys less energy than simply carrying a larger bank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Power Bank or Portable Power Station for Camping?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power bank charges small USB devices. A portable power station is a much larger battery with AC outlets, 12V sockets and hundreds of watt-hours. They solve different problems.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Power Bank Is Enough</h3>

          <p>
            If everything you bring charges by USB, phones, headlamps, lanterns, GPS units, cameras and most laptops, a power bank is lighter, cheaper and fits in a pocket. That covers backpacking and most tent camping.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Need a Power Station</h3>

          <p>
            CPAP machines, 12V fridges, electric blankets, string lights from a wall plug and anything else with an AC plug need a power station. See our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power stations for camping</a> for those.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When You Need a Generator</h3>

          <p>
            For RVs, air conditioners or a whole campsite's worth of power, a quiet inverter generator is the tool. Our <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/">camping generator guide</a> compares quiet options for campsites.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Flying to the Trailhead: The 100Wh Airline Rule
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your camping trip starts with a flight, the power bank's watt-hour rating decides whether it can come. The rules below are general guidance; check your airline before you fly, as carriers can set stricter limits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carry-On Only</h3>

          <p>
            Spare lithium batteries, including power banks, are generally not allowed in checked bags. Pack the bank in your carry-on and protect the ports from short circuits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Up to 100Wh Is Usually Allowed</h3>

          <p>
            Under common US and international rules, power banks up to 100Wh are allowed in carry-on bags without airline approval. Most handheld banks fall well under this. The Goal Zero Sherpa 100PD is listed at 95Wh, just inside the limit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">100Wh to 160Wh Needs Approval</h3>

          <p>
            Banks between 100 and 160Wh usually need airline approval and are limited in number; above 160Wh they are generally not allowed in passenger baggage. That rules out most power stations. If a bank lists only mAh, convert it using the formula above or look for the Wh printed on its case. Our <a href="/best-power-bank-for-travel/">travel power bank guide</a> covers flying in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging-at-camp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Charge Phones, Headlamps and GPS Units at Camp
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few habits stretch a power bank much further than buying a bigger one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pack the Right Cables</h3>

          <p>
            Bring one short cable for each connector you need, usually USB-C to USB-C and whatever your headlamp uses. Short cables lose less and tangle less. Check whether older lights need a USB-A port or an adapter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut the Phone's Drain</h3>

          <p>
            Airplane mode, low brightness and offline maps make a dramatic difference. A phone searching for a signal in the backcountry can burn through its battery on its own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Rechargeable Lights</h3>

          <p>
            A rechargeable <a href="/best-headlamp/">headlamp</a> uses a small fraction of a bank's capacity and saves carrying spare batteries. Top it up in the afternoon so it is full at dark.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charge in the Evening, Not All Night</h3>

          <p>
            Charge devices in the evening while you are awake, and keep the bank somewhere it cannot overheat. Do not bury a charging bank under insulation, and unplug devices once they are full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Keeping a Camping Power Bank Healthy Between Trips
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power bank that sits in a gear bin for months can be dead or degraded when you need it. A little care keeps it ready.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store It Partly Charged</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion batteries keep best when stored partly charged, often around half, rather than full or empty. Top it up a day or two before a trip and check it every few months in storage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Heat ages batteries faster than anything else. Do not leave a bank on a dashboard or in a closed car in summer sun, and keep it out of direct sun at camp.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Retire Damaged Banks</h3>

          <p>
            A bank that is swollen, cracked, unusually hot or smells odd should be taken out of service and recycled at a battery drop-off, not thrown in the trash. Follow the maker's safety instructions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. How Many Days Will a Power Bank Last While Camping?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It depends almost entirely on how you use your phone. Here is a practical way to estimate it before you pack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Estimate Your Daily Use</h3>

          <p>
            Note how much battery your phone uses on a typical outdoor day: in airplane mode with a few photos, it might use well under half; with navigation, video and a weak signal, it can use more than a full charge. Multiply by the number of days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match It to the Bank</h3>

          <p>
            Assume a bank delivers around two-thirds of its rated capacity as usable charge. Compare that with your phone's battery size and your daily use, then add a margin for cold, headlamps and the unexpected. If the numbers are tight, size up or bring a way to recharge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by mAh Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Milliamp-hours ignore voltage and conversion losses. Compare watt-hours where listed, and expect roughly two-thirds of rated capacity to reach your devices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Bank in a Cold Tent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bank left in a freezing vestibule loses usable capacity and may shut off. Keep it in your sleeping bag or an inside pocket.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on a Built-In Solar Panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small panels trickle-charge over days. Bring enough battery, or pair a regular bank with a folding solar panel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging With Wet Ports</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water in a port can cause shorts and corrosion, even on a rated bank. Dry it fully and close port plugs in the rain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Packing a Power Bank in Checked Luggage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spare lithium batteries generally must fly in carry-on bags. Check the Wh rating and your airline's rules before you leave for the trailhead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

