          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power bank for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most campers, the Nitecore NB20000 Gen 3 is the best power bank for camping. Its listing gives 20,000mAh at 10.26 ounces with an IPX5 rating and two USB-C ports. Backpackers should look at the 4.94-ounce Nitecore NB10000 Gen 4, and wet-weather campers at the IP67 Goal Zero Venture 75.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power bank for backpacking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The lightest bank that covers your trip. The Nitecore NB10000 Gen 4 weighs 4.94 ounces with 10,000mAh and IPX7, per the listing, which suits weekend trips. For longer stretches without resupply, the NB20000 Gen 3 doubles capacity at 10.26 ounces. Compare ounces per 10,000mAh rather than headline capacity alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many mAh do I need for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 10,000mAh covers a phone and headlamp for one to two nights. A long weekend of three or four nights suits 15,000 to 20,000mAh. For a week or more, a group, or cold weather, carry 20,000mAh or more, a second bank, or a folding solar panel to recharge during the trip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do power banks work in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They work, but with less usable capacity. Lithium-ion cells slow down below freezing and may shut off early. Keep the bank in an inside pocket by day and in your sleeping bag at night, and let it warm up before recharging, since charging a frozen lithium battery can damage it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a solar power bank good for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A built-in solar panel collects very little energy, so it is best treated as an emergency top-up. On trips longer than a week, a folding solar panel paired with a regular power bank recharges much faster. See our best solar power bank guide for panel and bank pairings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take a camping power bank on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes, in your carry-on bag only. Banks up to 100Wh are usually allowed without approval; 100 to 160Wh typically needs airline approval; larger batteries generally are not allowed. The Goal Zero Sherpa 100PD is listed at 95Wh. Always check your airline's rules before flying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are power banks waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most are not. Look for an IP rating: IPX5 resists rain and splashes, while IPX7 and IP67 survive brief submersion in standard tests. Ratings usually assume closed port plugs. Never charge a wet power bank, and dry the ports thoroughly first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
